1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,394
The word nice was once an insult.

2
00:00:02,394 --> 00:00:04,693
The word girl once meant a boy as well.

3
00:00:04,693 --> 00:00:10,704
And the letter b in debt was added on purpose, by scholars who wanted English to look more like Latin.

4
00:00:11,904 --> 00:00:14,792
English can look like a language with no rules.

5
00:00:14,792 --> 00:00:16,083
But it is not random.

6
00:00:16,083 --> 00:00:21,306
Every strange spelling and every odd word is a fossil, and each fossil tells a story.

7
00:00:22,505 --> 00:00:25,895
In the next twenty five minutes, we will dig up those fossils.

8
00:00:25,895 --> 00:00:32,291
Where your words came from, why they look the way they do, and how their stories can help you remember them for life.

9
00:00:33,491 --> 00:00:35,726
First, where English words come from.

10
00:00:35,726 --> 00:00:38,082
Second, why the spelling is so strange.

11
00:00:38,082 --> 00:00:40,981
And third, a four step method to learn any word.

12
00:00:40,981 --> 00:00:44,001
Stay for step four, because almost nobody does it.

13
00:00:45,201 --> 00:00:45,931
Chapter one.

14
00:00:45,931 --> 00:00:47,329
Three languages in one.

15
00:00:47,329 --> 00:00:49,456
English is not really one language.

16
00:00:49,456 --> 00:00:53,771
It is at least three, stacked on top of each other like layers of rock.

17
00:00:54,971 --> 00:01:00,444
The first layer is Old English, brought by the Anglo-Saxons from about the year four fifty.

18
00:01:00,444 --> 00:01:05,135
The second is French, which arrived with the Norman conquest in ten sixty six.

19
00:01:05,135 --> 00:01:12,052
The third is Latin and Greek, which poured in during the Renaissance, when scholars needed new words for new ideas.

20
00:01:13,252 --> 00:01:17,541
The oldest layer gives you the words closest to your heart and your home.

21
00:01:17,541 --> 00:01:18,892
House, bread and water.

22
00:01:18,892 --> 00:01:20,361
Mother, father and child.

23
00:01:20,361 --> 00:01:21,595
Eat, drink and sleep.

24
00:01:21,595 --> 00:01:23,592
They are short, strong and simple.

25
00:01:24,792 --> 00:01:29,797
In fact, the one hundred most common words in English are almost all from Old English.

26
00:01:29,797 --> 00:01:32,125
Words like the, and, you, have and good.

27
00:01:32,125 --> 00:01:34,919
You cannot build a single sentence without them.

28
00:01:36,119 --> 00:01:39,867
Then, in ten sixty six, William of Normandy conquered England.

29
00:01:39,867 --> 00:01:47,483
For about three hundred years, the kings, the judges and the nobles spoke French, while ordinary people kept speaking English.

30
00:01:48,683 --> 00:01:50,659
You can still taste that history.

31
00:01:50,659 --> 00:01:54,851
The animals in the field kept their English names: cow, pig and sheep.

32
00:01:54,851 --> 00:01:59,343
But on the lord's table, the meat took French names: beef, pork and mutton.

33
00:02:00,543 --> 00:02:03,029
French gave English its words of power.

34
00:02:03,029 --> 00:02:05,196
Government, court, judge and jury.

35
00:02:05,196 --> 00:02:06,280
Army and soldier.

36
00:02:06,280 --> 00:02:08,447
Prison, tax, crown and parliament.

37
00:02:08,447 --> 00:02:12,909
If a word sounds official, there is a good chance it came from French.

38
00:02:14,110 --> 00:02:16,587
And this is where English becomes rich.

39
00:02:16,587 --> 00:02:20,082
Very often, it kept all three layers for the same idea.

40
00:02:20,082 --> 00:02:21,480
Kingly is Old English.

41
00:02:21,480 --> 00:02:22,496
Royal is French.

42
00:02:22,496 --> 00:02:23,449
Regal is Latin.

43
00:02:23,449 --> 00:02:26,689
Three words, one meaning, three different feelings.

44
00:02:27,890 --> 00:02:29,113
Here is another one.

45
00:02:29,113 --> 00:02:31,865
To ask is Old English, and it feels friendly.

46
00:02:31,865 --> 00:02:34,862
To question is French, and it feels more serious.

47
00:02:34,862 --> 00:02:38,592
To interrogate is Latin, and it sounds like a police station.

48
00:02:39,792 --> 00:02:42,422
So here is your first lesson as a learner.

49
00:02:42,422 --> 00:02:45,302
Short Old English words sound warm and direct.

50
00:02:45,302 --> 00:02:47,869
Long Latin words sound formal and clever.

51
00:02:47,869 --> 00:02:50,687
Good speakers choose between them on purpose.

52
00:02:51,887 --> 00:02:54,227
With a friend, say help, start and buy.

53
00:02:54,227 --> 00:02:57,888
In a formal email, you can say assist, commence and purchase.

54
00:02:57,888 --> 00:02:59,808
Same meanings, different layers.

55
00:02:59,808 --> 00:03:02,568
Now you know why English has so many synonyms.

56
00:03:03,768 --> 00:03:15,239
One well known survey of the dictionary found that more than half of English words come from French and Latin, about a quarter from the Germanic family, and the rest from Greek and many other languages.

57
00:03:16,439 --> 00:03:19,629
And there is a fourth layer, hiding inside the first.

58
00:03:19,629 --> 00:03:24,985
From the seven hundreds, Vikings from Scandinavia raided England, and then settled there.

59
00:03:24,985 --> 00:03:27,995
Their language, Old Norse, mixed with Old English.

60
00:03:29,195 --> 00:03:31,693
The Vikings gave English sky, egg and knife.

61
00:03:31,693 --> 00:03:36,007
They gave it window, which meant wind eye, a hole in the roof for the smoke.

62
00:03:36,007 --> 00:03:38,788
They even gave it the words they, them and their.

63
00:03:39,988 --> 00:03:42,332
Sometimes English kept both versions.

64
00:03:42,332 --> 00:03:44,233
Shirt is the Old English word.

65
00:03:44,233 --> 00:03:46,133
Skirt is its Old Norse cousin.

66
00:03:46,133 --> 00:03:51,075
They began as the same word, and ended up as two different pieces of clothing.

67
00:03:52,275 --> 00:03:55,828
You can even see the layers side by side in legal English.

68
00:03:55,828 --> 00:03:58,524
Lawyers still write last will and testament.

69
00:03:58,524 --> 00:04:00,300
Will is the Old English word.

70
00:04:00,300 --> 00:04:01,955
Testament is the Latin one.

71
00:04:01,955 --> 00:04:05,324
Two words, one meaning, sitting together for centuries.

72
00:04:06,524 --> 00:04:11,800
And during the Renaissance, thousands of learned words arrived from Latin and Greek.

73
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:12,428
Education.

74
00:04:12,428 --> 00:04:12,994
Skeleton.

75
00:04:12,994 --> 00:04:13,685
Atmosphere.

76
00:04:13,685 --> 00:04:14,438
Thermometer.

77
00:04:14,438 --> 00:04:19,275
When science needed a new name, it almost always went to these two languages.

78
00:04:20,475 --> 00:04:23,369
Add all these layers together, and you get a giant.

79
00:04:23,369 --> 00:04:28,817
The Oxford English Dictionary lists about one hundred and seventy thousand words in current use.

80
00:04:28,817 --> 00:04:34,889
The good news is that you only need a small fraction of them, and the layers tell you which ones to choose.

81
00:04:36,090 --> 00:04:38,610
But three languages were only the beginning.

82
00:04:38,610 --> 00:04:40,786
Because English did something unusual.

83
00:04:40,786 --> 00:04:45,139
Wherever it travelled, it collected words, from hundreds of other languages.

84
00:04:45,139 --> 00:04:47,774
And some of them came from very close to home.

85
00:04:48,974 --> 00:04:49,706
Chapter two.

86
00:04:49,706 --> 00:04:50,989
Words that travelled.

87
00:04:50,989 --> 00:04:52,394
English is a collector.

88
00:04:52,394 --> 00:04:58,012
Sailors, traders, soldiers and scientists carried words home from every corner of the world.

89
00:04:59,212 --> 00:05:00,656
Our first stop is Arabic.

90
00:05:00,656 --> 00:05:08,336
During the Middle Ages, Arabic was a great language of science, medicine and trade, and Europe borrowed its knowledge, and its words.

91
00:05:09,536 --> 00:05:18,782
Algebra comes from al jabr, a word in the title of a famous book by the mathematician al Khwarizmi, written in Baghdad in the ninth century.

92
00:05:18,782 --> 00:05:21,754
His own name gave us another word: algorithm.

93
00:05:22,954 --> 00:05:25,520
Zero comes from sifr, which means empty.

94
00:05:25,520 --> 00:05:27,060
Sugar comes from sukkar.

95
00:05:27,060 --> 00:05:28,536
Cotton comes from qutn.

96
00:05:28,536 --> 00:05:32,257
And magazine comes from makhazin, which means storehouses.

97
00:05:32,257 --> 00:05:37,006
A magazine was first a storehouse, and later, a storehouse of information.

98
00:05:38,206 --> 00:05:40,150
Some words made long journeys.

99
00:05:40,150 --> 00:05:42,290
Coffee began as the Arabic qahwa.

100
00:05:42,290 --> 00:05:44,040
In Turkish it became kahve.

101
00:05:44,040 --> 00:05:45,207
In Italian, caffe.

102
00:05:45,207 --> 00:05:47,282
And finally, in English, coffee.

103
00:05:47,282 --> 00:05:50,978
One word, four languages, and you drink it every morning.

104
00:05:52,178 --> 00:05:55,335
So if you are an Arabic speaker, here is some good news.

105
00:05:55,335 --> 00:05:57,646
You already know dozens of English words.

106
00:05:57,646 --> 00:05:59,958
You just did not know that you knew them.

107
00:06:01,158 --> 00:06:02,579
Our second stop is India.

108
00:06:02,579 --> 00:06:09,684
When British traders and soldiers lived there, they borrowed words from Hindi and Urdu for things they had never seen before.

109
00:06:10,884 --> 00:06:14,815
Shampoo comes from a word that meant to press, or to massage.

110
00:06:14,815 --> 00:06:17,135
Jungle comes from jangal, wild land.

111
00:06:17,135 --> 00:06:20,163
And a bungalow was a house in the Bengal style.

112
00:06:20,163 --> 00:06:22,419
Pyjamas and loot came the same way.

113
00:06:23,619 --> 00:06:26,251
From the Americas came chocolate and tomato.

114
00:06:26,251 --> 00:06:32,594
Both started in Nahuatl, the language of the Aztecs, passed through Spanish, and then sailed into English.

115
00:06:33,794 --> 00:06:35,310
Tea comes from Chinese.

116
00:06:35,310 --> 00:06:36,958
Piano comes from Italian.

117
00:06:36,958 --> 00:06:40,386
Kindergarten is German, and means children's garden.

118
00:06:40,386 --> 00:06:43,089
And tsunami is Japanese for harbour wave.

119
00:06:43,089 --> 00:06:44,539
English kept them all.

120
00:06:45,739 --> 00:06:47,429
Where does robot come from?

121
00:06:47,429 --> 00:06:47,804
Japan?

122
00:06:47,804 --> 00:06:47,992
No.

123
00:06:47,992 --> 00:06:51,435
It comes from a Czech play, written in nineteen twenty.

124
00:06:51,435 --> 00:06:54,564
It was made from robota, a word for forced labour.

125
00:06:55,764 --> 00:06:58,128
Some words even come from people's names.

126
00:06:58,128 --> 00:07:02,451
The sandwich is named after the Earl of Sandwich, in the seventeen sixties.

127
00:07:02,451 --> 00:07:06,083
The story says he wanted to eat without leaving the card table.

128
00:07:07,283 --> 00:07:14,166
And in eighteen eighty, in Ireland, a land agent named Captain Charles Boycott was shut out by his whole community.

129
00:07:14,166 --> 00:07:16,620
Nobody would work for him or sell to him.

130
00:07:16,620 --> 00:07:19,074
Within weeks, his name had become a verb.

131
00:07:20,274 --> 00:07:22,853
So English is a museum of world languages.

132
00:07:22,853 --> 00:07:29,056
Every borrowed word is a small postcard from history, telling you who met whom, and what they traded.

133
00:07:30,256 --> 00:07:31,646
And the list goes on.

134
00:07:31,646 --> 00:07:33,233
Lemon comes from laymun.

135
00:07:33,233 --> 00:07:35,417
Syrup comes from sharab, a drink.

136
00:07:35,417 --> 00:07:37,137
Giraffe comes from zarafa.

137
00:07:37,137 --> 00:07:39,850
And admiral comes from amir, a commander.

138
00:07:41,050 --> 00:07:43,152
Even the night sky speaks Arabic.

139
00:07:43,152 --> 00:07:51,688
Many of the brightest stars still carry Arabic names, like Altair, Aldebaran and Vega, written down by Arab astronomers centuries ago.

140
00:07:52,888 --> 00:07:55,657
Closer to home, the neighbours lent words too.

141
00:07:55,657 --> 00:07:58,546
From Dutch sailors came yacht, skipper and deck.

142
00:07:58,546 --> 00:08:01,676
From French kitchens came restaurant, menu and chef.

143
00:08:01,676 --> 00:08:04,444
And from Italy came opera, violin and balcony.

144
00:08:05,644 --> 00:08:06,994
And it never stopped.

145
00:08:06,994 --> 00:08:09,566
Emoji is Japanese for picture character.

146
00:08:09,566 --> 00:08:12,331
It has nothing to do with the word emotion.

147
00:08:12,331 --> 00:08:14,260
Karaoke means empty orchestra.

148
00:08:14,260 --> 00:08:17,990
And tycoon comes from a Japanese title meaning great lord.

149
00:08:19,190 --> 00:08:21,512
So English takes words from everyone?

150
00:08:21,512 --> 00:08:22,767
And gives them back.

151
00:08:22,767 --> 00:08:23,394
Like what?

152
00:08:23,394 --> 00:08:28,164
Internet, computer and weekend are now used in languages all over the world.

153
00:08:29,364 --> 00:08:33,658
From Persian came bazaar, a market, and caravan, a group of travellers.

154
00:08:33,658 --> 00:08:37,529
And paradise comes from an old Persian word for a walled garden.

155
00:08:37,529 --> 00:08:41,581
For people of the desert, a garden with walls and water was heaven.

156
00:08:42,782 --> 00:08:46,102
And every time you say checkmate, you are speaking Persian.

157
00:08:46,102 --> 00:08:50,323
It comes from shah mat, which is usually explained as the king is helpless.

158
00:08:50,323 --> 00:08:53,250
The game of chess carried the word across the world.

159
00:08:54,450 --> 00:08:56,760
That explains where the words came from.

160
00:08:56,760 --> 00:09:00,111
But it does not explain the strangest thing about English.

161
00:09:00,111 --> 00:09:02,249
Why on earth is it spelled like that?

162
00:09:03,449 --> 00:09:04,255
Chapter three.

163
00:09:04,255 --> 00:09:05,464
The spelling mystery.

164
00:09:05,464 --> 00:09:06,903
Why does knight have a k?

165
00:09:06,903 --> 00:09:08,227
Why does debt have a b?

166
00:09:08,227 --> 00:09:11,336
And why does one letter make so many different sounds?

167
00:09:12,536 --> 00:09:13,984
How do you say this word?

168
00:09:13,984 --> 00:09:14,854
Just say night.

169
00:09:14,854 --> 00:09:16,418
Then why all those letters?

170
00:09:16,418 --> 00:09:19,953
Because, a thousand years ago, people really pronounced them.

171
00:09:21,153 --> 00:09:25,091
In Old English, the word was cniht, and every letter was spoken.

172
00:09:25,091 --> 00:09:29,952
Over the centuries, the k sound and the rough gh sound disappeared from speech.

173
00:09:29,952 --> 00:09:34,074
But the spelling stayed, like a photograph of an old pronunciation.

174
00:09:35,274 --> 00:09:38,000
Then came two events at almost the same time.

175
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:46,418
In fourteen seventy six, William Caxton set up the first printing press in England, and printers slowly began to fix the spelling of words.

176
00:09:46,418 --> 00:09:50,052
But while spelling was freezing, pronunciation was changing.

177
00:09:51,252 --> 00:09:53,514
Linguists call it the Great Vowel Shift.

178
00:09:53,514 --> 00:10:01,997
Between about fourteen hundred and seventeen hundred, over three hundred years, the long vowels of English slowly moved to new positions in the mouth.

179
00:10:03,197 --> 00:10:06,104
Before the shift, the word bite sounded like beet.

180
00:10:06,104 --> 00:10:07,500
House sounded like hoos.

181
00:10:07,500 --> 00:10:09,069
And name sounded like nahm.

182
00:10:09,069 --> 00:10:13,430
Today they sound completely different, but we still spell them the old way.

183
00:10:14,630 --> 00:10:16,630
So English spelling is not crazy.

184
00:10:16,630 --> 00:10:17,236
It is old.

185
00:10:17,236 --> 00:10:20,449
It shows you how words sounded six hundred years ago.

186
00:10:20,449 --> 00:10:24,570
When the spelling and the sound do not match, do not blame yourself.

187
00:10:24,570 --> 00:10:25,418
Blame history.

188
00:10:26,618 --> 00:10:29,502
But some silent letters are not old sounds at all.

189
00:10:29,502 --> 00:10:30,367
They are fakes.

190
00:10:30,367 --> 00:10:37,577
In the fifteen hundreds, scholars loved Latin so much that they added letters to English words to show off their Latin roots.

191
00:10:38,777 --> 00:10:42,679
The word debt came from French, and it was spelled d e t t e.

192
00:10:42,679 --> 00:10:46,197
Scholars added a b, because the Latin word was debitum.

193
00:10:46,197 --> 00:10:48,308
The same thing happened to doubt.

194
00:10:48,308 --> 00:10:50,674
Nobody ever pronounced those letters.

195
00:10:51,874 --> 00:10:53,876
And sometimes they got it wrong.

196
00:10:53,876 --> 00:10:56,941
Island was an Old English word, with no s at all.

197
00:10:56,941 --> 00:11:01,131
Scholars thought it came from the Latin insula, so they added an s.

198
00:11:01,131 --> 00:11:01,819
It did not.

199
00:11:01,819 --> 00:11:05,009
The s in island is a five hundred year old mistake.

200
00:11:06,209 --> 00:11:07,822
So what should a learner do?

201
00:11:07,822 --> 00:11:08,571
Three things.

202
00:11:08,571 --> 00:11:11,047
Always learn the sound of a new word first.

203
00:11:11,047 --> 00:11:13,524
Then learn its spelling as a separate fact.

204
00:11:13,524 --> 00:11:17,786
And group words with the same pattern, like night, light, right and fight.

205
00:11:18,986 --> 00:11:22,096
Think of silent letters as a boss in a video game.

206
00:11:22,096 --> 00:11:23,899
You beat it with three moves.

207
00:11:23,899 --> 00:11:24,708
Listen first.

208
00:11:24,708 --> 00:11:26,200
Learn in pattern groups.

209
00:11:26,200 --> 00:11:28,004
And say every new word aloud.

210
00:11:29,204 --> 00:11:30,738
Could we fix the spelling?

211
00:11:30,738 --> 00:11:32,627
People have tried for centuries.

212
00:11:32,627 --> 00:11:34,575
But whose accent would we choose?

213
00:11:34,575 --> 00:11:38,352
A Londoner, a Texan and an Australian all say water differently.

214
00:11:38,352 --> 00:11:41,421
The old spelling is strange, but everyone shares it.

215
00:11:42,621 --> 00:11:44,355
Why is there an h in ghost?

216
00:11:44,355 --> 00:11:50,201
Many of Caxton's printers came from Flanders, and in Flemish the word was spelled with g h.

217
00:11:50,201 --> 00:11:52,257
They added the h, and it stayed.

218
00:11:53,457 --> 00:11:55,782
And then there are the letters o u g h.

219
00:11:55,782 --> 00:11:56,199
Though.

220
00:11:56,199 --> 00:11:56,676
Through.

221
00:11:56,676 --> 00:11:57,034
Tough.

222
00:11:57,034 --> 00:11:57,392
Cough.

223
00:11:57,392 --> 00:12:00,074
The same four letters, four different sounds.

224
00:12:00,074 --> 00:12:03,413
Each one is a fossil from a different old pronunciation.

225
00:12:04,613 --> 00:12:07,561
The k in knee, knife and know was once pronounced.

226
00:12:07,561 --> 00:12:09,860
So was the w in write, wrong and wrist.

227
00:12:09,860 --> 00:12:14,164
And the g h in night, light and daughter was a rough sound in the throat.

228
00:12:15,364 --> 00:12:18,035
One man did manage to change some spellings.

229
00:12:18,035 --> 00:12:23,682
In eighteen twenty eight, Noah Webster published his American dictionary, with simpler forms.

230
00:12:23,682 --> 00:12:28,904
That is why Americans write color and center, and the British write colour and centre.

231
00:12:30,104 --> 00:12:31,783
Some patterns do help you.

232
00:12:31,783 --> 00:12:34,495
Look at the letter e at the end of a word.

233
00:12:34,495 --> 00:12:35,593
Hop becomes hope.

234
00:12:35,593 --> 00:12:36,691
Bit becomes bite.

235
00:12:36,691 --> 00:12:37,789
Cut becomes cute.

236
00:12:37,789 --> 00:12:41,148
That silent e tells you the vowel before it is long.

237
00:12:42,348 --> 00:12:44,977
And strange spelling has one real advantage.

238
00:12:44,977 --> 00:12:46,890
Knight and night sound the same.

239
00:12:46,890 --> 00:12:48,982
So do write and right, sea and see.

240
00:12:48,982 --> 00:12:53,584
On the page, the old spelling tells you instantly which one the writer means.

241
00:12:54,784 --> 00:12:57,420
So spelling froze while the sounds moved on.

242
00:12:57,420 --> 00:13:01,435
But something else was changing too, and this one is even stranger.

243
00:13:01,435 --> 00:13:03,652
The meanings of the words themselves.

244
00:13:04,852 --> 00:13:05,659
Chapter four.

245
00:13:05,659 --> 00:13:07,584
Words that changed their minds.

246
00:13:07,584 --> 00:13:08,950
A word is not a stone.

247
00:13:08,950 --> 00:13:13,421
It is alive, and over centuries, its meaning can travel a very long way.

248
00:13:14,621 --> 00:13:15,570
Start with nice.

249
00:13:15,570 --> 00:13:18,953
It comes from the Latin nescius, which means not knowing.

250
00:13:18,953 --> 00:13:23,937
When it entered English around thirteen hundred, a nice person was a foolish person.

251
00:13:25,137 --> 00:13:26,520
Then it slowly changed.

252
00:13:26,520 --> 00:13:27,783
From foolish, to shy.

253
00:13:27,783 --> 00:13:29,768
From shy, to careful and precise.

254
00:13:29,768 --> 00:13:35,541
And only around the seventeen hundreds did it arrive at the meaning you know: pleasant and kind.

255
00:13:36,741 --> 00:13:37,699
You are so nice!

256
00:13:37,699 --> 00:13:40,093
In thirteen hundred, that was an insult.

257
00:13:40,093 --> 00:13:41,470
Then, you are so silly?

258
00:13:41,470 --> 00:13:43,924
In twelve hundred, that was a compliment!

259
00:13:45,124 --> 00:13:45,810
It is true.

260
00:13:45,810 --> 00:13:49,929
Silly comes from an Old English word that meant blessed and happy.

261
00:13:49,929 --> 00:13:51,801
Then it came to mean innocent.

262
00:13:51,801 --> 00:13:53,111
Then weak and simple.

263
00:13:53,111 --> 00:13:54,422
And finally, foolish.

264
00:13:54,422 --> 00:13:55,233
Nice went up.

265
00:13:55,233 --> 00:13:56,232
Silly went down.

266
00:13:57,432 --> 00:13:59,222
Some meanings became narrower.

267
00:13:59,222 --> 00:14:02,326
In Middle English, a girl was a child of either sex.

268
00:14:02,326 --> 00:14:06,086
And meat meant any kind of food, not only the flesh of animals.

269
00:14:07,286 --> 00:14:08,806
Look at awful and awesome.

270
00:14:08,806 --> 00:14:12,488
Both were born from the word awe, a feeling of fear and wonder.

271
00:14:12,488 --> 00:14:14,183
Awful once meant full of awe.

272
00:14:14,183 --> 00:14:17,456
Today, one twin is terrible, and the other is wonderful.

273
00:14:18,656 --> 00:14:20,157
And here is my favourite.

274
00:14:20,157 --> 00:14:22,620
A clue was once a clew, a ball of thread.

275
00:14:22,620 --> 00:14:27,424
In the old Greek story, a hero used a thread to find his way out of a labyrinth.

276
00:14:27,424 --> 00:14:30,788
So a clue became anything that guides you to the answer.

277
00:14:31,988 --> 00:14:33,747
Hidden pictures are everywhere.

278
00:14:33,747 --> 00:14:37,324
Muscle comes from the Latin musculus, which means little mouse.

279
00:14:37,324 --> 00:14:41,752
The Romans thought a moving muscle looked like a mouse running under the skin.

280
00:14:42,952 --> 00:14:45,777
Quarantine comes from the Italian for forty days.

281
00:14:45,777 --> 00:14:51,948
In the time of the plague, ships arriving in Venice had to wait forty days before anyone could come ashore.

282
00:14:53,148 --> 00:14:57,939
Disaster means a bad star, from a time when people blamed the stars for bad luck.

283
00:14:57,939 --> 00:15:01,193
Breakfast is the meal that breaks your night long fast.

284
00:15:01,193 --> 00:15:03,914
And goodbye is a short form of God be with ye.

285
00:15:05,114 --> 00:15:06,945
Now, stop and notice something.

286
00:15:06,945 --> 00:15:10,074
You have just heard the stories of about fifty words.

287
00:15:10,074 --> 00:15:12,672
And you will remember many of them tomorrow.

288
00:15:12,672 --> 00:15:12,908
Why?

289
00:15:12,908 --> 00:15:14,857
Because your brain loves stories.

290
00:15:16,057 --> 00:15:19,205
A companion is, literally, someone you share bread with.

291
00:15:19,205 --> 00:15:23,028
It comes from the Latin com, meaning with, and panis, meaning bread.

292
00:15:23,028 --> 00:15:26,120
A company was first a group of people who ate together.

293
00:15:27,320 --> 00:15:31,879
A candidate was a Roman who wore a bright white toga when asking for votes.

294
00:15:31,879 --> 00:15:34,858
Salary is linked to sal, the Latin word for salt.

295
00:15:34,858 --> 00:15:36,986
Panic comes from the Greek god Pan.

296
00:15:36,986 --> 00:15:40,390
And school comes from a Greek word that meant free time.

297
00:15:41,590 --> 00:15:43,806
Some everyday words are small poems.

298
00:15:43,806 --> 00:15:47,867
A daisy is the day's eye, because the flower opens in the morning.

299
00:15:47,867 --> 00:15:51,498
A nightmare was a spirit that sat on sleepers in the night.

300
00:15:51,498 --> 00:15:55,621
And alphabet is simply alpha and beta, the first two Greek letters.

301
00:15:56,821 --> 00:15:59,288
And a villain was not a criminal at all.

302
00:15:59,288 --> 00:16:01,755
The word first meant a poor farm worker.

303
00:16:01,755 --> 00:16:06,504
Rich people looked down on farm workers, so the word slowly became an insult.

304
00:16:06,504 --> 00:16:07,737
Words can be unfair.

305
00:16:08,937 --> 00:16:11,602
Even hello has a story, and it is a young one.

306
00:16:11,602 --> 00:16:15,715
In the early eighteen hundreds, it was mostly a shout to get attention.

307
00:16:15,715 --> 00:16:22,029
Then the telephone arrived, people needed a word to start a call, and hello became the greeting of the world.

308
00:16:23,229 --> 00:16:25,294
And words love to get shorter.

309
00:16:25,294 --> 00:16:26,602
Omnibus became bus.

310
00:16:26,602 --> 00:16:28,185
Telephone became phone.

311
00:16:28,185 --> 00:16:29,631
Influenza became flu.

312
00:16:29,631 --> 00:16:31,765
And refrigerator became fridge.

313
00:16:31,765 --> 00:16:35,276
When people use a word every day, they cut it down.

314
00:16:36,476 --> 00:16:38,569
This is the big idea of this video.

315
00:16:38,569 --> 00:16:40,304
Do not learn words as a list.

316
00:16:40,304 --> 00:16:41,620
Learn them as stories.

317
00:16:41,620 --> 00:16:43,594
And that brings us to the method.

318
00:16:44,794 --> 00:16:45,530
Chapter five.

319
00:16:45,530 --> 00:16:46,719
The four step method.

320
00:16:46,719 --> 00:16:51,870
This is how to use everything you have just seen to learn any English word, and to keep it.

321
00:16:53,070 --> 00:16:53,591
Step one.

322
00:16:53,591 --> 00:16:54,400
Find the root.

323
00:16:54,400 --> 00:16:58,099
Remember, more than half of English comes from Latin and French.

324
00:16:58,099 --> 00:17:02,088
That means thousands of words are built from a small number of roots.

325
00:17:03,288 --> 00:17:04,550
Take the root port.

326
00:17:04,550 --> 00:17:06,410
In Latin, it means to carry.

327
00:17:06,410 --> 00:17:08,203
Transport: to carry across.

328
00:17:08,203 --> 00:17:09,598
Export: to carry out.

329
00:17:09,598 --> 00:17:10,927
Import: to carry in.

330
00:17:10,927 --> 00:17:12,853
Portable: able to be carried.

331
00:17:12,853 --> 00:17:14,780
Support: to carry from below.

332
00:17:15,980 --> 00:17:17,679
The root spect means to look.

333
00:17:17,679 --> 00:17:19,554
Inspect: to look into something.

334
00:17:19,554 --> 00:17:22,776
Respect: to look at someone again, because they matter.

335
00:17:22,776 --> 00:17:25,530
And a spectator is someone who looks at a game.

336
00:17:26,730 --> 00:17:29,869
Dict means to say: dictionary, predict, dictate.

337
00:17:29,869 --> 00:17:33,073
Tele means far: telephone, television, telescope.

338
00:17:33,073 --> 00:17:34,447
Graph means to write.

339
00:17:34,447 --> 00:17:35,428
Bio means life.

340
00:17:35,428 --> 00:17:38,436
So a biography is simply a life, written down.

341
00:17:39,636 --> 00:17:41,527
English builds words like Lego.

342
00:17:41,527 --> 00:17:45,493
A prefix at the front, a root in the middle, a suffix at the end.

343
00:17:45,493 --> 00:17:46,591
Un, believe, able.

344
00:17:46,591 --> 00:17:47,384
Unbelievable.

345
00:17:47,384 --> 00:17:51,533
Learn the pieces, and you can open words you have never seen before.

346
00:17:52,733 --> 00:17:54,881
Here are four tools to start with.

347
00:17:54,881 --> 00:17:55,703
Un means not.

348
00:17:55,703 --> 00:17:56,651
Re means again.

349
00:17:56,651 --> 00:17:57,851
Less means without.

350
00:17:57,851 --> 00:17:59,241
And ful means full of.

351
00:17:59,241 --> 00:18:03,222
With only these four, you can understand hundreds of new words.

352
00:18:04,422 --> 00:18:04,952
Step two.

353
00:18:04,952 --> 00:18:05,894
Learn the story.

354
00:18:05,894 --> 00:18:09,486
When you meet a new word, spend thirty seconds on its origin.

355
00:18:09,486 --> 00:18:12,842
Most good dictionaries show it, under the word etymology.

356
00:18:14,042 --> 00:18:15,958
A story gives your memory a hook.

357
00:18:15,958 --> 00:18:20,836
You will not forget that muscle is a little mouse, or that a disaster is a bad star.

358
00:18:20,836 --> 00:18:23,913
A plain translation gives your brain nothing to hold.

359
00:18:25,113 --> 00:18:29,010
So in your notebook, do not write only the word and its translation.

360
00:18:29,010 --> 00:18:33,938
Write the word, how it sounds, its story in one line, and then a sentence of your own.

361
00:18:35,138 --> 00:18:35,817
Step three.

362
00:18:35,817 --> 00:18:36,249
Use it.

363
00:18:36,249 --> 00:18:39,025
A word you only read is a word you will lose.

364
00:18:39,025 --> 00:18:43,281
Within one day, put the new word into a sentence about your own life.

365
00:18:44,481 --> 00:18:45,785
New word: portable.

366
00:18:45,785 --> 00:18:46,951
Sentence, please.

367
00:18:46,951 --> 00:18:50,107
My laptop is portable, but my homework is not.

368
00:18:50,107 --> 00:18:50,725
Accepted.

369
00:18:51,925 --> 00:18:54,309
Make the sentence true, and make it yours.

370
00:18:54,309 --> 00:19:01,348
A real sentence about your room, your family or your day ties the word to your own life, and that makes the memory stronger.

371
00:19:02,548 --> 00:19:03,692
And now, step four.

372
00:19:03,692 --> 00:19:05,378
The step almost nobody does.

373
00:19:05,378 --> 00:19:07,486
Review the word, at the right time.

374
00:19:07,486 --> 00:19:12,184
This one step can decide whether a word stays with you for a day, or for life.

375
00:19:13,384 --> 00:19:18,984
In eighteen eighty five, a German psychologist named Hermann Ebbinghaus tested his own memory.

376
00:19:18,984 --> 00:19:23,989
He found that we forget new information very quickly at first, and then more slowly.

377
00:19:23,989 --> 00:19:26,134
This is called the forgetting curve.

378
00:19:27,334 --> 00:19:33,713
But every time you review a word just before you forget it, the memory becomes stronger and lasts longer.

379
00:19:33,713 --> 00:19:35,839
So review a new word after one day.

380
00:19:35,839 --> 00:19:37,176
Then after three days.

381
00:19:37,176 --> 00:19:38,269
Then after a week.

382
00:19:38,269 --> 00:19:39,424
Then after a month.

383
00:19:40,624 --> 00:19:42,570
This is called spaced repetition.

384
00:19:42,570 --> 00:19:46,344
And the important rule is this: do not just read the word again.

385
00:19:46,344 --> 00:19:47,170
Test yourself.

386
00:19:47,170 --> 00:19:49,646
Cover the meaning, and try to remember it.

387
00:19:49,646 --> 00:19:52,713
The effort of remembering is what builds the memory.

388
00:19:53,913 --> 00:19:55,716
So here is the whole method.

389
00:19:55,716 --> 00:19:56,618
Find the root.

390
00:19:56,618 --> 00:19:57,648
Learn the story.

391
00:19:57,648 --> 00:19:59,451
Use it in your own sentence.

392
00:19:59,451 --> 00:20:01,512
And review it at the right time.

393
00:20:01,512 --> 00:20:03,122
Root, story, use, review.

394
00:20:04,322 --> 00:20:06,639
And which words should you learn first?

395
00:20:06,639 --> 00:20:14,897
Research on vocabulary suggests that about three thousand common word families cover around ninety five percent of everyday spoken English.

396
00:20:16,097 --> 00:20:21,092
A word family is a word with its relatives, like help, helpful, helpless and helper.

397
00:20:21,092 --> 00:20:27,098
So start with the most common families, and learn them deeply, before you chase rare and fancy words.

398
00:20:28,298 --> 00:20:30,228
And meet your words in the wild.

399
00:20:30,228 --> 00:20:31,494
Read things you love.

400
00:20:31,494 --> 00:20:33,242
Watch with English subtitles.

401
00:20:33,242 --> 00:20:34,267
Listen every day.

402
00:20:34,267 --> 00:20:39,513
You need to meet a word many times, in different places, before it truly becomes yours.

403
00:20:40,713 --> 00:20:42,382
Here is a simple daily plan.

404
00:20:42,382 --> 00:20:44,527
Five minutes: review your old words.

405
00:20:44,527 --> 00:20:48,461
Five minutes: learn three new words, with their roots and stories.

406
00:20:48,461 --> 00:20:50,070
Then write three sentences.

407
00:20:50,070 --> 00:20:53,229
And finish with five minutes of reading or listening.

408
00:20:54,429 --> 00:20:56,926
Let us try the whole method on one word.

409
00:20:56,926 --> 00:20:58,361
The word is manuscript.

410
00:20:58,361 --> 00:21:00,234
Maybe you have seen it before.

411
00:21:00,234 --> 00:21:00,858
Maybe not.

412
00:21:00,858 --> 00:21:03,105
Watch how the four steps open it up.

413
00:21:04,305 --> 00:21:05,554
Step one, the roots.

414
00:21:05,554 --> 00:21:10,488
Manu means hand, as in manual, and manufacture, which first meant made by hand.

415
00:21:10,488 --> 00:21:13,861
Script means written, as in describe and prescription.

416
00:21:13,861 --> 00:21:16,672
So a manuscript is something written by hand.

417
00:21:17,872 --> 00:21:19,066
Step two, the story.

418
00:21:19,066 --> 00:21:25,812
Before the printing press, every single book in the world was a manuscript, copied out by hand, letter by letter.

419
00:21:25,812 --> 00:21:29,513
Today, writers still call their unpublished book a manuscript.

420
00:21:30,713 --> 00:21:32,229
Step three, your sentence.

421
00:21:32,229 --> 00:21:35,379
My grandfather kept an old manuscript in a wooden box.

422
00:21:35,379 --> 00:21:37,886
Make yours true, or at least make it yours.

423
00:21:37,886 --> 00:21:41,736
Step four, review it tomorrow, then in three days, then next week.

424
00:21:42,936 --> 00:21:44,349
And look what happened.

425
00:21:44,349 --> 00:21:49,940
You learned one word, but you also unlocked manual, manufacture, describe and prescription.

426
00:21:49,940 --> 00:21:51,599
That is the power of roots.

427
00:21:51,599 --> 00:21:52,828
One key, many doors.

428
00:21:54,028 --> 00:21:55,034
One more secret.

429
00:21:55,034 --> 00:21:56,920
Words have favourite partners.

430
00:21:56,920 --> 00:21:59,121
We say heavy rain, not strong rain.

431
00:21:59,121 --> 00:22:01,762
We make a mistake, we do not do a mistake.

432
00:22:01,762 --> 00:22:03,523
So never learn a word alone.

433
00:22:03,523 --> 00:22:05,221
Learn it with its partners.

434
00:22:06,421 --> 00:22:10,719
And remember the difference between words you understand and words you use.

435
00:22:10,719 --> 00:22:13,240
Reading and listening build the first group.

436
00:22:13,240 --> 00:22:16,334
Only speaking and writing move a word into the second.

437
00:22:17,534 --> 00:22:21,303
And when you forget a word, do not call yourself bad at languages.

438
00:22:21,303 --> 00:22:22,502
Forgetting is normal.

439
00:22:22,502 --> 00:22:24,272
It is part of how memory works.

440
00:22:24,272 --> 00:22:27,298
Just review it again, and it will come back stronger.

441
00:22:28,498 --> 00:22:30,840
Three more keys for your collection.

442
00:22:30,840 --> 00:22:33,963
Vis and vid mean to see: vision, visible, video.

443
00:22:33,963 --> 00:22:37,021
Aud means to hear: audio, audience, auditorium.

444
00:22:37,021 --> 00:22:40,209
And scrib means to write: describe, and scribble.

445
00:22:41,409 --> 00:22:43,977
When you learn the sound, learn the stress too.

446
00:22:43,977 --> 00:22:46,326
A record, the noun, is strong at the start.

447
00:22:46,326 --> 00:22:48,620
To record, the verb, is strong at the end.

448
00:22:48,620 --> 00:22:51,898
So in your notebook, mark the strong part of every new word.

449
00:22:53,098 --> 00:22:55,933
Three new words a day does not sound like much.

450
00:22:55,933 --> 00:22:59,190
But in one year, that is more than one thousand words.

451
00:22:59,190 --> 00:23:00,216
Learned properly.

452
00:23:00,216 --> 00:23:01,422
Remembered for life.

453
00:23:02,622 --> 00:23:05,651
Before you go, let us do step four together.

454
00:23:05,651 --> 00:23:06,546
A quick quiz.

455
00:23:06,546 --> 00:23:07,578
Five questions.

456
00:23:07,578 --> 00:23:09,506
Answer out loud before I do.

457
00:23:10,706 --> 00:23:11,504
Question one.

458
00:23:11,504 --> 00:23:12,303
Cow and beef.

459
00:23:12,303 --> 00:23:13,961
Which one came from French?

460
00:23:13,961 --> 00:23:14,636
It is beef.

461
00:23:14,636 --> 00:23:15,926
The farmers said cow.

462
00:23:15,926 --> 00:23:17,953
The lords at the table said beef.

463
00:23:19,153 --> 00:23:19,903
Question two.

464
00:23:19,903 --> 00:23:22,676
What did nice mean in the year thirteen hundred?

465
00:23:22,676 --> 00:23:23,658
It meant foolish.

466
00:23:23,658 --> 00:23:25,738
From the Latin nescius, not knowing.

467
00:23:26,938 --> 00:23:27,837
Question three.

468
00:23:27,837 --> 00:23:31,015
Which letter in the word island was added by mistake?

469
00:23:31,015 --> 00:23:31,794
The letter s.

470
00:23:31,794 --> 00:23:34,313
Scholars wrongly linked the word to Latin.

471
00:23:35,513 --> 00:23:36,402
Question four.

472
00:23:36,402 --> 00:23:38,245
What does the root port mean?

473
00:23:38,245 --> 00:23:39,389
It means to carry.

474
00:23:39,389 --> 00:23:42,439
Transport, export, import, portable and support.

475
00:23:43,639 --> 00:23:44,683
And question five.

476
00:23:44,683 --> 00:23:46,656
When should you review a new word?

477
00:23:46,656 --> 00:23:49,614
After one day, three days, one week, and one month.

478
00:23:49,614 --> 00:23:51,702
If you got three or more, well done.

479
00:23:51,702 --> 00:23:53,617
You are already using the method.

480
00:23:54,817 --> 00:23:57,450
Before we finish, three mistakes to avoid.

481
00:23:57,450 --> 00:24:00,147
Do not memorise long lists with no context.

482
00:24:00,147 --> 00:24:01,526
Do not only translate.

483
00:24:01,526 --> 00:24:05,477
And never learn a word once and walk away without reviewing it.

484
00:24:06,677 --> 00:24:08,420
Let us look back at the road.

485
00:24:08,420 --> 00:24:10,825
Old English gave the language its heart.

486
00:24:10,825 --> 00:24:12,088
French gave it power.

487
00:24:12,088 --> 00:24:14,012
Latin and Greek gave it science.

488
00:24:14,012 --> 00:24:15,876
The whole world gave it colour.

489
00:24:15,876 --> 00:24:18,702
And today, it is your turn to add to the story.

490
00:24:19,902 --> 00:24:22,714
Every word you learn was carried to you by someone.

491
00:24:22,714 --> 00:24:24,809
A farmer, a sailor, a scholar, a poet.

492
00:24:24,809 --> 00:24:27,511
When you learn it, you become part of that chain.

493
00:24:28,711 --> 00:24:30,238
So here is your homework.

494
00:24:30,238 --> 00:24:31,948
Pick one English word today.

495
00:24:31,948 --> 00:24:32,864
Find its story.

496
00:24:32,864 --> 00:24:36,406
And write it in the comments, with a sentence of your own.

497
00:24:37,606 --> 00:24:39,862
A word with a story is a word you keep.

498
00:24:39,862 --> 00:24:45,706
If this video helped you, subscribe to Mastering English Library, and I will see you in the next one.

